85 #edb/ref 1082 #edb/ref 17592186045498 #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186371985 #edb/ref 1083 #uuid "3bfc894d-49bf-586f-9c15-0545c41d8169" #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186371986 #edb/ref 1080 "20x+2y{x}^{3}-2x{y}^{2}" #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186371986 #edb/ref 1082 #edb/ref 17592186045498 #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186371986 #edb/ref 1083 #uuid "79846a78-79c8-5a37-ae61-84fe6a556fc5" #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186371987 #edb/ref 1080 "20x+{x}^{3}-2x{y}^{2}" #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186371987 #edb/ref 1082 #edb/ref 17592186045498 #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186371987 #edb/ref 1083 #uuid "c5c3b3f1-d211-5651-a1bd-4e92f6df7b3c" #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186405069 #edb/ref 1068 #edb/ref 17592186405070 #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186405069 #edb/ref 1069 "q-161896" #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186405069 #edb/ref 1070 #edb/ref 17592186045485 #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186405069 #edb/ref 1071 "![](images/14/1422e6534c001214335dcddb8888f7e2e9344e440379c47da38514e9a3ef0bd8.png)\n\nConsider the vector field $F=⟨z,\\,0,\\,2⟩$ and the closed surface $S$ that represents the boundary of the solid shown above. Let $D$ be the projection of $S$ onto the $xy$-plane. The flux of $F$ across $S$ can be expressed in terms of a double integral as \n\n$$\n{∬}_{S}F\\cdot dS={∬}_{D}\\,\\,\\text{d}A.\n$$\n\nWhich expression belongs in the blank when the upper and lower surfaces are parametrized as graphs over $D$ and their outward flux integrands are added pointwise where defined, before integration?" #edb/ref 17592186045423] [17592186405069 #edb/ref 1072 #uuid "0d7ee053-a1f8-527b-9332-a3761ff9b930" #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186405069 #edb/ref 1074 "Since we have a closed surface, the positive orientation is outward-pointing by convention. So, \n\n- the upper surface ${S}_{1}:z=g(x,y)=1-\\sqrt{{x}^{2}+{y}^{2}}$ is oriented upward, and\n\n- the lower surface ${S}_{2}:z=h(x,y)=0$ is oriented downward.\n\nNow, we write down the flux across each of the surfaces separately.\n\n- Consider the upper cone ${S}_{1}.$\n\nWe compute the partial derivatives of $z=g(x,y):$\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\\frac{∂g}{∂x} & =\\frac{∂}{∂x}(1-\\sqrt{{x}^{2}+{y}^{2}})=-\\frac{x}{\\sqrt{{x}^{2}+{y}^{2}}} \\\\ \\frac{∂g}{∂y} & =\\frac{∂}{∂y}(1-\\sqrt{{x}^{2}+{y}^{2}})=-\\frac{y}{\\sqrt{{x}^{2}+{y}^{2}}}\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nIf we denote $F=⟨P,\\,Q,\\,R⟩,$ then\n\n$$\nP=z=1-\\sqrt{{x}^{2}+{y}^{2}},\\,Q=0,\\,R=2.\n$$\n\n \n\nAs a result, since the positive orientation is upward, we have\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{∬}_{{S}_{1}}F\\cdot \\text{d}S & ={∬}_{D}(-P\\frac{∂g}{∂x}-Q\\frac{∂g}{∂y}+R)\\text{d}A \\\\  & ={∬}_{D}-(1-\\sqrt{{x}^{2}+{y}^{2}})\\cdot (-\\frac{x}{\\sqrt{{x}^{2}+{y}^{2}}})-0\\cdot (-\\frac{y}{\\sqrt{{x}^{2}+{y}^{2}}})+2\\,\\text{d}A \\\\  & ={∬}_{D}\\frac{x}{\\sqrt{{x}^{2}+{y}^{2}}}-x+2\\,\\text{d}A.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\n- Consider the lower plane ${S}_{2}.$ \n\nSince the positive orientation is downward, the unit normal vector to the plane is $n=⟨0,0,-1⟩.$ As a result, we have\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{∬}_{{S}_{2}}F\\cdot \\text{d}S & ={∬}_{{S}_{2}}F\\cdot n\\,\\text{d}S \\\\  & ={∬}_{D}⟨z,0,2⟩\\cdot ⟨0,0,-1⟩\\,\\text{d}A \\\\  & ={∬}_{D}-2\\,\\text{d}A.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nTherefore, the flux of $F$ across the entire surface is \n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{∬}_{S}F\\cdot \\text{d}S & ={∬}_{{S}_{1}}F\\cdot \\text{d}S+{∬}_{{S}_{2}}F\\cdot \\text{d}S \\\\  & ={∬}_{D}\\frac{x}{\\sqrt{{x}^{2}+{y}^{2}}}-x+2\\,\\text{d}A+{∬}_{D}-2\\,\\text{d}A \\\\  & ={∬}_{D}\\frac{x}{\\sqrt{{x}^{2}+{y}^{2}}}-x\\,\\text{d}A.\\end{aligned}\n$$" #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186405070 #edb/ref 1075 #edb/ref 17592186405071 #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186405070 #edb/ref 1075 #edb/ref 17592186405072 #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186405070 #edb/ref 1075 #edb/ref 17592186405073 #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186405070 #edb/ref 1075 #edb/ref 17592186405074 #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186405070 #edb/ref 1075 #edb/ref 17592186405075 #edb/ref 17592186045417] [